#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639} :activity/id #uuid "39af6225-7719-5f09-ae8c-6df89ff6b7cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048280 :topic/id #uuid "900f65f4-ad2b-51fa-b027-f2b2cba3065b" :topic/math-academy-id 3754} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180661 :tutorial/content "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} ,\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}.\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." :tutorial/id #uuid "73bca8be-ae46-5214-9ba6-2beb553be49f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2082 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bdf65be2-4038-5c62-b8e0-2362d10939cc" :step/math-academy-id 7964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179496 :question/id #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" :question/math-academy-id "e-2333"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid